On Sat, 3 Apr 2004 10:51:34 +1000, Stephan Ahonen wrote
(in message <_rWdnfoZuYvHl_PdRVn-sA@comcast.com>):

> How's this for an idea. The latest fad in programming nowadays 

Hardly the latest (started in the 1960s), not a fad. [More common 
with recent machines because of the computing power required for 
non-trivial problems.]

> seems to be making bots that learn how to do stuff by themselves 
> by simply letting them apply random output to a system, and 
> whichever random output has the best effect on the system, use 
> that as a launching point for the next iteration, etc, a sort of 
> evolutionary model. 

Genetic Algorithm that determines the topology and (initial) 
weightings of a Neural Network.

While it may be possible to construct such a thing (given 
near-unlimited time & computing power) for NetHack, it seems a long 
way from feasible to me. [My knowledge in the area is admittedly 
limited - I'm having enough trouble figuring out how to build such 
a thing for a 13-card, 3-box variant of poker for 2-4 players... 
which looks to be a much simpler task than the equivalent for 
NetHack]

> So why not toss one of these "learnng models" into nethack and 

Typically you'd throw thousands of them, discard the worst x%, 
breed the best y% to produce (100-x-y)% new offspring... wash, 
rinse, repeat. 

> let it run around for a while, using the score when it dies as 
> the basis for judging how well it's doing. A few conditionals to 
> let it know the special significance of stuff like the Amulet of 
> Yendor and ascending, and you're all set. 

Which conditionals? The only easy answer is 'none', making the 
problem much harder, choices beyond that will dramatically affect 
whether and how quickly viable bots will appear.

Would you hard-wire solutions to Sokoban levels? If so what happens 
when a momster moves or destroys a boulder (giants, or /oStriking)

Do you stick to a single role/gender/race combination for each 
population? [about 73 race/gender/role combinations =73 
populations] Or do you assume a good strategy is one which works 
for any combination, and allow your 'good chaotic ranger' to breed 
with a 'good knight' to produce a 'hopefully good Monk'?

It is not clear to me that the score of a died-early bot would be a 
good measure of fitness. [some other measure may be better suited, 
is all I'm saying - some combination of score, XL, AC, HP, DL might 
make a better index]


> I am not a programmer, so feel free to flame me if I'm 
